From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 12:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] tick: don't report a hard lockup on CPUs frozen in s2idle

The buddy hardlockup detector decides a CPU is wedged purely from its
hrtimer_interrupts counter not advancing:

	if (per_cpu(hrtimer_interrupts_saved, cpu) == hrint)
		return true;

Every CPU parked in a suspend-to-idle freeze has legitimately stopped
advancing that counter, and nothing clears the state on the way out.
tick_unfreeze() -> timekeeping_resume() calls touch_softlockup_watchdog(),
which covers the softlockup detector only; the hardlockup side is never
touched.

So on a long s2idle sleep the first CPU to take a wakeup eventually runs
its own watchdog hrtimer for the third time, calls
watchdog_buddy_check_hardlockup() -> watchdog_hardlockup_check() against
the next CPU, finds a counter that has not moved, and reports a hard
lockup on a CPU that is merely still asleep. With
CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_HARDLOCKUP_PANIC=y that is fatal.

Observed on a msm8998 phone (Pixel 2 XL) as a reboot 115-866 s into every
s2idle sleep, with no log surviving the reset. Setting
kernel.hardlockup_panic=0 -- which changes nothing about the hardware --
made the same sleep run to completion, proving no CPU was ever stuck.

Suspend-to-RAM does not hit this: disable_nonboot_cpus() takes the other
CPUs out through watchdog_hardlockup_disable(), which touches the buddy as
it goes. s2idle keeps every CPU online, so only the freeze path can do it.

Mark every online CPU touched on unfreeze. This has to happen on every
unfreeze rather than once per suspend, because the touch is consumed by
the first watchdog_hardlockup_check() that observes it and a long s2idle
sleep unfreezes once per wakeup.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 kernel/time/tick-common.c | 29 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+)

diff --git a/kernel/time/tick-common.c b/kernel/time/tick-common.c
index 6a9198a4279b..e57d09ab06c9 100644
--- a/kernel/time/tick-common.c
+++ b/kernel/time/tick-common.c
@@ -547,6 +547,33 @@ void tick_freeze(void)
 	raw_spin_unlock(&tick_freeze_lock);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The buddy hardlockup detector decides a CPU is wedged purely from its
+ * hrtimer_interrupts not advancing, and every CPU in an s2idle freeze has
+ * legitimately stopped advancing it. Mark them all touched on the way out, so
+ * the first CPU to wake cannot report a hard lockup against a CPU that is
+ * merely still asleep.
+ *
+ * Suspend-to-RAM never hits this: disable_nonboot_cpus() takes the other CPUs
+ * out through watchdog_hardlockup_disable(), which touches as it goes. s2idle
+ * keeps them online, so only this path can.
+ *
+ * This has to run on every unfreeze, not once per suspend: the touch is
+ * consumed by the first watchdog_hardlockup_check() that sees it, and a long
+ * s2idle sleep unfreezes once per wakeup.
+ */
+#if defined(CONFIG_HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR_COUNTS_HRTIMER)
+static void tick_unfreeze_touch_hardlockup(void)
+{
+	unsigned int cpu;
+
+	for_each_online_cpu(cpu)
+		watchdog_hardlockup_touch_cpu(cpu);
+}
+#else
+static inline void tick_unfreeze_touch_hardlockup(void) { }
+#endif
+
 /**
  * tick_unfreeze - Resume the local tick and (possibly) timekeeping.
  *
@@ -571,6 +598,8 @@ void tick_unfreeze(void)
 		sched_clock_resume();
 		lock_map_release(&tick_freeze_map);
 
+		tick_unfreeze_touch_hardlockup();
+
 		system_state = SYSTEM_RUNNING;
 		trace_suspend_resume(TPS("timekeeping_freeze"),
 				     smp_processor_id(), false);
